C = [
("h1", "8. First Corinthians 15 in Its Place"),
("p", "Claim: the second pairing of Adam and Christ stands in a chapter written to answer one question, and the question is not the one Romans 5 answers. Paul introduces the two men in 1 Corinthians 15:21 as support for a statement about the resurrection of the dead. This chapter reads verses 1 to 20, so that when the two men appear, the reader knows what they are there to prove."),
("sub", "8.1 The gospel declared (15:1–11)"),
("p", "Paul begins by putting the Corinthians in mind of something. “Moreover, brethren, I declare unto you the gospel which I preached unto you, which also ye have received, and wherein ye stand” (1 Corinthians 15:1). He says that by it they “are saved, if ye keep in memory what I preached unto you, unless ye have believed in vain” (1 Corinthians 15:2). The chapter is written to people who have heard the gospel, received it and stand in it, and Paul now recalls its first elements."),
("p", "He gives them in order. “I delivered unto you first of all that which I also received, how that Christ died for our sins according to the scriptures; And that he was buried, and that he rose again the third day according to the scriptures” (1 Corinthians 15:3–4). The list of witnesses follows: Cephas, then the twelve, then “above five hundred brethren at once,” then James, then “all the apostles,” and “last of all he was seen of me also, as of one born out of due time” (1 Corinthians 15:5–8). The record is of a risen man who was seen."),
("p", "Paul adds his own place in it. “For I am the least of the apostles, that am not meet to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the church of God” (1 Corinthians 15:9). “But by the grace of God I am what I am” (1 Corinthians 15:10). The passage ends the account with a statement of agreement: “so we preach, and so ye believed” (1 Corinthians 15:11). Paul and the other witnesses preach the same thing about the risen Christ, and the Corinthians believed it."),
("sub", "8.2 The denial (15:12)"),
("p", "Then comes the reason for the chapter. “Now if Christ be preached that he rose from the dead, how say some among you that there is no resurrection of the dead?” (1 Corinthians 15:12). Some among the Corinthians say that there is no resurrection of the dead. Paul does not say why they say it, and the volume does not guess. He takes the denial as the fact that the chapter must answer, and he answers it by reasoning from the resurrection of Christ that they have already accepted."),
("sub", "8.3 The argument from the dead (15:13–19)"),
("p", "The argument is a chain of conditional sentences. If there is no resurrection of the dead, then Christ is not risen. If Christ is not risen, preaching is vain, faith is vain, the witnesses are false, and those who have died in Christ have perished. Paul draws the conclusion in the sentence that ends the chain: “If in this life only we have hope in Christ, we are of all men most miserable” (1 Corinthians 15:19)."),
("ctbl", "The chain of 15:13–19.", [
    ("Verse", "If", "Then"),
    ("15:13", "there be no resurrection of the dead", "“then is Christ not risen”"),
    ("15:14", "Christ be not risen", "“then is our preaching vain, and your faith is also vain”"),
    ("15:15", "the dead rise not", "“we are found false witnesses of God”"),
    ("15:17", "Christ be not raised", "“your faith is vain; ye are yet in your sins”"),
    ("15:18", "(the same condition)", "“they also which are fallen asleep in Christ are perished”"),
    ("15:19", "in this life only we have hope in Christ", "“we are of all men most miserable”"),
  ], [0.12, 0.38, 0.50]),
("p", "The chain has one beginning and one end. It begins with the denial that there is a resurrection of the dead. It ends with a hope that is limited to this life. In between, Paul puts the three things that depend on the resurrection: the preaching, the faith and the dead who are asleep. The chapter is not a chapter about sin and its removal in the way Romans 5 is, though sin is mentioned (“ye are yet in your sins,” 15:17). It is a chapter about whether the dead will rise."),
("sub", "8.4 “But now is Christ risen” (15:20)"),
("p", "“But now is Christ risen from the dead, and become the firstfruits of them that slept” (1 Corinthians 15:20). Verse 20 breaks the chain with two words. “But now.” After the long sequence of “if,” Paul states the fact. Christ is risen. He has “become the firstfruits of them that slept.”"),
("p", "The word “firstfruits” is taken from the harvest. The firstfruits are the first part of the crop to ripen, and they are brought in as a pledge of the rest. By calling Christ the firstfruits of “them that slept,” Paul says that his rising is the first of a harvest, and that those who have fallen asleep belong to the crop of which he is the first part. The next verse gives the reason in terms of two men."),
("sub", "8.5 How the chapter turns to Adam"),
("p", "The turn to Adam is carried by a single word. “For since by man came death, by man came also the resurrection of the dead” (1 Corinthians 15:21). The “for” ties the verse to verse 20. Christ is the firstfruits of the dead, and the reason that this should be so is that death came by a man and so it is fitting that the resurrection of the dead should come by a man."),
("p", "The point of the pairing, in this chapter, is plain. Paul is not arguing about how sin entered the world or about the extent of the judgment. He is arguing that a resurrection of the dead is to be expected, since death came by a man and the resurrection comes by another man. The question the passage answers is the question the Corinthians had put in doubt."),
("tbl", "The movement of 1 Corinthians 15:12–21.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("1Cor 15:12", "“how say some among you that there is no resurrection of the dead?”", "The denial that the chapter must answer"),
    ("1Cor 15:19", "“If in this life only we have hope in Christ, we are of all men most miserable.”", "The end of the chain of “if”"),
    ("1Cor 15:20", "“But now is Christ risen from the dead, and become the firstfruits of them that slept”", "The fact stated against the chain"),
    ("1Cor 15:21", "“For since by man came death, by man came also the resurrection of the dead”", "The first man and the second, brought in to support the fact"),
    ("1Cor 15:22", "“For as in Adam all die, even so in Christ shall all be made alive”", "The sentence that holds both men"),
  ], [0.16, 0.50, 0.34]),
("p", "The chapter’s place in the letter has one more feature. Paul’s letter to Corinth deals with many matters, and he takes up this one near the end. When he comes to it he does not begin with Adam. He begins with the gospel and the witnesses, and he reaches the two men only after he has stated that Christ is risen. The two men are an argument, brought in after the fact has been laid down. In Romans 5 the two men are the whole subject of the passage. In 1 Corinthians 15 they are two verses in an argument whose subject is the resurrection."),
]
